// tests/kernel/drivers/wifi/hw_smoke_wifi_placeholder.pdx — R38.M7-005 (#1322)
//
// DORMANT hardware-gated placeholder for the R38.M7 WiFi feature
// smoke on a real ThinkPad T14 G4.
//
// #1322 delivers `tools/hw-smoke-r38-wifi.md`: an operator recipe for
// asserting that the R38.M7 wire (HE-MCS rate table + PER-driven
// rate control + roaming reassoc + rekey rotation) works against a
// real AX211 radio, a real AP and a real over-the-air envelope.  Per
// design/roadmap/next-wave-synthesis.md §10 D7 (gated:hardware
// discipline), an issue whose acceptance requires PHYSICAL hardware
// in the loop CANNOT emit a golden fingerprint from a QEMU-OVMF
// boot -- QEMU has no CNVi transport, no PHY to program a rate on,
// no neighbouring APs to roam to, and no live 4-way handshake to
// rekey, and the fingerprint would lie.
//
// The RIGHT SHAPE for a dormant issue is a placeholder module that
// COMPILES but emits NOTHING.  This module is that placeholder.  It
// carries no witness function, no fingerprint, and no boot-witness
// registration.  The build-time coverage gate is satisfied because
// there is nothing to cover.
//
// When R38.M8+ or a later milestone lands the WiFi feature smoke on
// real hardware, this file is replaced by a real synth that emits
// "WIFI HW-SMOKE OK" and takes ITS marker into the golden.
//
// The empty module is deliberately unreferenced from
// r30_platform.pdx.  That is the whole point of the dormant shape:
// nothing at boot calls into it, so nothing at boot lies about it.

module HwSmokeWifiPlaceholder = structure {
  // Compile-only marker so paideia-as accepts an "empty" module.
  pub let _r38_m7_005_hw_smoke_wifi_placeholder : u64 = 0
}
